<body>

<header>
  <div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div>
  <h1>One Problem, Many Ideas</h1>
  <div class="sub">Week 4 · Day 1 — Unit III: Idea Generation &amp; Validation</div>
</header>

<div class="timestrip">
  <div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div>
  <div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Choral Recall</div>
  <div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>DI Instruction</div>
  <div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Idea List / SPED Pull-Aside</div>
  <div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Logoff</div>
</div>

<div class="wrap">

<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  Students generate multiple ideas against the real need they named last week — not just pick their favorite. This is the first step toward the canvas functioning as a "dashboard of hypotheses" rather than one fixed plan.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Pull out your Week 3 paragraph. Read your own sentence to yourself — "Near me, it is hard to..." That's your starting point today. What is an unmet need?</p>
  <p class="cr">"A problem that's not already being solved well."</p>
  <p class="t">Today we don't pick one idea yet. We make a bunch.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — I Do / We Do / You Do</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — One Problem, Multiple Solutions</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Here's a trap a lot of first-time entrepreneurs fall into — they find a real problem, and they jump straight to the first idea that comes to mind. That's risky. A <span class="k">hypothesis</span> is just a guess you haven't tested yet — and your first idea is only one guess out of many possible ones.</p>
  <p class="a">Model with your own Week 3-style need: e.g., "elders need rides to appointments." Generate three different possible solutions on the board — a scheduled ride service, a volunteer driver sign-up board, a partnership with an existing local service. Same problem, three different guesses.</p>
  <p class="t">Notice — I didn't pick one yet. I just proved there's more than one way to solve the same problem.</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">We Do — Stretching for More Ideas</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Call out — for my example, what's a fourth idea, even a weird one?</p>
  <p class="a">Take 2–3 more, including at least one deliberately unconventional suggestion. Praise the weird ones too — quantity matters more than quality at this stage.</p>
  <p class="t">The goal isn't to find the perfect idea today. It's to prove to yourself you're capable of more than one guess.</p>
</div>

<div class="note channel">
  <span class="label">Learning Channel</span>
  Think/Say (rapid brainstorm) — deliberately fast-paced and low-stakes so students don't self-censor. Slower, evaluative thinking comes Day 2, not today.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">You Do — Guided Practice</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Using your own Week 3 need, write at least three different possible ideas that could solve it. Don't judge them yet — just get three down.</p>
</div>

<div class="note adhd">
  <span class="label">ADHD Observation Tip</span>
  Brainstorming can stall if a student fixates on judging their first idea before writing a second one. A simple external rule — "three ideas before you're allowed to reread any of them" — removes the decision point rather than relying on a verbal reminder each time.
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Practice — Two Tracks Running Concurrently</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Independent Practice</h3>
<p>Students finish their list of at least three ideas. Early finishers: circle the one that feels most interesting right now — not final, just a starting lean for Day 2.</p>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "Is It a Business, or a Favor?"</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">If you help your neighbor carry groceries for free, is that a business?</p>
    <p class="s">[no]</p>
    <p class="t">Right — that's a favor. What would make it a business instead?</p>
    <p class="s">[getting paid / money]</p>
    <p class="t">A business needs money to change hands. A favor doesn't. Both are good things to do — but only one is a business.</p>
    <p class="a">Use two picture cards — "Favor" (a handshake, no money icon) and "Business" (money changing hands) — and sort several simple scenarios (mowing a lawn for free vs. for $10; helping a friend move vs. running a moving service) into the correct column.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">One sentence: name one of your three ideas from today.</p>
  <p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent per student.</p>
</div>

<table class="assess">
  <tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="identity">
  <h3>Your First Idea Was Never the Only One</h3>
  <p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. Having more than one idea isn't a sign you're unsure — it's a sign you're thinking like a real entrepreneur. Tomorrow we figure out how to actually test which one holds up.</p>
</div>

</div>
</body>
