<header>
  <div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div>
  <h1>Test Before You Build</h1>
  <div class="sub">Week 4 · Day 2 — Validation and the Minimum Viable Product</div>
</header>

<div class="timestrip">
  <div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div>
  <div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Choral Recall</div>
  <div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>DI Instruction</div>
  <div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Validation Plan / SPED Pull-Aside</div>
  <div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Logoff</div>
</div>

<div class="wrap">

<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  Students learn lightweight ways to test an idea before committing to it — the core of the Lean Startup approach and the Backwards Design goal of "validating, not just picking a favorite." Financial literacy touch: II. Spending — testing small first protects limited resources.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Yesterday — a hypothesis is?</p>
  <p class="cr">"A guess you haven't tested yet."</p>
  <p class="t">And a favor versus a business — what changes it?</p>
  <p class="cr">"Money changes hands."</p>
  <p class="t">Today we learn how to test a guess before you spend real money on it.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — I Do / We Do / You Do</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — Rock'N Outlaw Started Small</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Remember Rock'N Outlaw from Week 1? They didn't start with a 30 by 60 foot building and a feed segment. They started with flowers, perfume, and jewelry — small, low-risk products. That wasn't just being modest. That was <span class="k">testing</span>.</p>
  <p class="t">A small first version of your idea, just big enough to find out whether people really want it, is called a <span class="k">minimum viable product</span> — an <span class="k">MVP</span>. Say it with me:</p>
  <p class="cr">"An MVP is the smallest version that still tests the idea."</p>
  <p class="t">If people had not bought those first small products, Rock'N Outlaw would have found that out cheaply — before borrowing money for a big building.</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">We Do — Four Ways to Test an Idea</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Before you build anything big, there are simple ways to check whether your idea holds up. I'll name them, you repeat them:</p>
  <p class="cr">"Ask people. Watch people. Try a small version. Look at what already exists."</p>
  <p class="a">Give one quick example of each using your own community-need example from Day 1 (e.g., ask three elders if a ride service would help; watch how they currently get rides; run one trial week with a sign-up sheet; look at whether any existing service already covers it).</p>
  <p class="t">Notice — none of those cost much money. That's the point.</p>
</div>

<div class="note channel">
  <span class="label">Learning Channel</span>
  Hear/Say (four-method chant) into See/Do (concrete example for each) — the chant gives a memorable frame, the example makes each method actionable rather than abstract.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">You Do — Guided Practice</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Circle the idea you're leaning toward from yesterday. Now write which two of the four testing methods you could actually do this week, and how.</p>
</div>

<div class="note comprehension">
  <span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>
  Watch for students who name a testing method but describe something that isn't actually a test (e.g., "ask my friends" when the friends would say yes just to be nice). Push with "would they tell you if it was a bad idea?" — validating means seeking honest answers, not agreement.
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Practice — Two Tracks Running Concurrently</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Validation Plan</h3>
<p>Students finish their two-method testing plan. Early finishers: who specifically would you ask, and what exact question would you ask them?</p>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "Try a Little First"</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">Before you buy a lot of something, it's smart to try a little first. If you want to sell cookies, would you bake 100 cookies first, or just a few?</p>
    <p class="s">[a few]</p>
    <p class="t">Why?</p>
    <p class="s">[in case nobody buys them / it costs less]</p>
    <p class="t">That's what testing means. Try a little first. See if people want it.</p>
    <p class="a">Use picture cards — a small tray of cookies and a giant tray of cookies. Students point to which one is the smarter first step, and explain why in their own words.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">One sentence: name one way you could test your idea this week without spending much money.</p>
  <p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent per student.</p>
</div>

<table class="assess">
  <tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="identity">
  <h3>Starting Small Is Smart, Not Small</h3>
  <p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. Rock'N Outlaw started with flowers and ended up building a bigger space. Starting small wasn't a weakness — it was the strategy. You have everything you need to start the same way.</p>
</div>

</div>
