<header>
  <div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div>
  <h1>Changing Direction Without Quitting</h1>
  <div class="sub">Week 4 · Day 3 — Precision Teaching Fluency Day + the Pivot</div>
</header>

<div class="timestrip">
  <div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div>
  <div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Choral Recall</div>
  <div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>Pivot Mini-Lesson + Fluency Rounds</div>
  <div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Round 3 / SPED Pull-Aside</div>
  <div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Logoff</div>
</div>

<div class="wrap">

<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  Precision Teaching day — fluency (accuracy + speed) on Week 4 vocabulary: hypothesis, validate, MVP, pivot, unmet need. "Pivot" is new today, so it gets a short I Do first. Financial literacy touch: II. Spending — pivoting early costs less than pushing a failing idea.
</div>

<div class="note iefa">
  <span class="label">IEFA Connection</span>
  Per the pacing map, no forced IEFA anchor this week. If a local example of a business that changed direction comes to mind (a family or community venture that shifted what it offered based on what customers wanted), it fits naturally in the pivot mini-lesson.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Quick fire. Hypothesis?</p>
  <p class="cr">"A guess you haven't tested yet."</p>
  <p class="t">MVP?</p>
  <p class="cr">"The smallest version that still tests the idea."</p>
  <p class="t">Today we add one more word, then get fast with all of them.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · Pivot Mini-Lesson + Fluency Rounds</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — What a Pivot Is</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Say you test your idea and people say, "Not really what I need." Two choices: quit, or <span class="k">pivot</span>. A pivot means keeping the problem you care about, but changing your solution based on what you learned. Say it with me:</p>
  <p class="cr">"A pivot is changing direction because of what you learned."</p>
  <p class="t">Example: someone tests a ride service for elders and finds out the real problem isn't the ride, it's that nobody remembers to call and schedule one. So they pivot to a reminder-and-scheduling service instead. Same problem, different solution.</p>
  <p class="t">A pivot isn't failing. Failing is not testing at all and finding out too late.</p>
</div>

<div class="note comprehension">
  <span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>
  Students often hear "pivot" as "give up." Check for this directly: ask "did the person in my example quit, or keep going?" The answer matters more than the vocabulary definition.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">Precision Teaching — Three-Round Fluency Structure</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="a"><span class="k">Round 1 — With Support:</span> Word wall visible (hypothesis, validate, MVP, pivot, unmet need). Call a term, students respond chorally, checking wall as needed.</p>
  <p class="a"><span class="k">Round 2 — Without Support:</span> Cover the word wall. Timed — 60 seconds. Call terms rapid-fire, individual students respond, log speed and accuracy.</p>
</div>

<div class="fluency-table">
  <tr><th>Round</th><th>Support</th><th>Timing</th><th>Response Mode</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1</td><td>Word wall visible</td><td>Untimed</td><td>Choral</td></tr>
  <tr><td>2</td><td>None</td><td>60 sec, timed</td><td>Individual, cold-call</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3</td><td>None</td><td>Untimed</td><td>Peer partner quiz</td></tr>
</div>

<div class="note channel">
  <span class="label">Learning Channel</span>
  Hear/Say (chant) then Think/Say (explaining the pivot example back) — hearing the definition and then re-explaining it in their own words is what separates memorized from understood.
</div>

<div class="note adhd">
  <span class="label">ADHD Observation Tip</span>
  By the fourth fluency day, the routine should run mostly on its own. If a student who was calm in earlier rounds is now blurting or checked out, that's worth noticing — the routine hasn't changed, so something else may be going on that day.
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Round 3 — Peer Identification / SPED Pull-Aside</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Round 3</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Partner up, quiz each other on all five terms. If your partner's stuck, ask a question that helps them find the answer.</p>
  <p class="a">Circulate and log Not Yet/Developing/Fluent per student based on Rounds 2 and 3 combined.</p>
</div>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "Try Something Different"</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level. Untimed, repeated practice reps.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">You try selling lemonade. Nobody buys it. What could you do?</p>
    <p class="s">[quit / try something else]</p>
    <p class="t">You could try something different, like selling water on a hot day. You still want to sell drinks. You just changed what you sell. That's a pivot.</p>
    <p class="a">Use picture cards — lemonade, water bottle, a sun. Have students act out or point through the steps: try it, learn something, change it.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Fast round, no writing: I say a term, you say the definition.</p>
</div>

<table class="assess">
  <tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="identity">
  <h3>Changing Direction Is Not Quitting</h3>
  <p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. Learning something and changing course because of it isn't weakness — it's the whole skill. You have everything you need to keep going, even when the first idea doesn't hold. Tomorrow you test yours.</p>
</div>

</div>
