<header>
  <div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div>
  <h1>Testing My Idea</h1>
  <div class="sub">Week 4 · Day 4 — Running a Lightweight Validation</div>
</header>

<div class="timestrip">
  <div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div>
  <div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Choral Recall</div>
  <div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>DI Instruction</div>
  <div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Run the Test / SPED Pull-Aside</div>
  <div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Logoff</div>
</div>

<div class="wrap">

<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  Students put the validation plan from Day 2 into action and record what they learn — the raw evidence that feeds Friday's validated-idea artifact. Financial literacy touch: II. Spending — a quick test costs almost nothing.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Four ways to test an idea — say them with me.</p>
  <p class="cr">"Ask people. Watch people. Try a small version. Look at what already exists."</p>
  <p class="t">And a pivot is?</p>
  <p class="cr">"Changing direction because of what you learned."</p>
  <p class="t">Today you actually run a test.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — Running a Test</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — Asking a Question That Gets an Honest Answer</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Here's where a lot of tests go wrong. If I ask "Wouldn't a ride service be great?" — nearly everyone says yes. That question hands them the answer. Watch me fix it.</p>
  <p class="a">Model on the board: weak question ("Wouldn't this be great?") versus strong questions ("How do you get to appointments right now?" and "What's the hardest part about that?"). Point out that the strong questions ask about their real life, not their opinion of your idea.</p>
  <p class="t">Best test question of all — <span class="k">would you actually pay for this, and how much?</span> Saying yes is free. Paying isn't.</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">We Do — Fixing Weak Questions</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">I'll read a question. Thumbs up if it will get an honest answer, thumbs down if it just fishes for a yes.</p>
  <p class="a">Read: "You'd love this, right?" (down) / "How do you handle this problem today?" (up) / "Isn't that a good idea?" (down) / "What would you pay for something that fixes this?" (up). Discuss any split votes and have students rewrite the weak ones.</p>
</div>

<div class="note channel">
  <span class="label">Learning Channel</span>
  See/Say (thumbs sort) into Think/Write (rewriting a weak question) — students evaluate someone else's question before writing their own, which is easier than starting from a blank page.
</div>

<div class="note comprehension">
  <span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>
  Watch for students who rewrite a weak question by only changing a few words while it still leads the person to yes. Check whether the new question asks about the person's real life or still asks for an opinion of the idea.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">You Do — Guided Practice</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Write three honest questions you could ask a real person about your need, plus one "would you pay" question. Then ask at least two real people — classmates, family, or community members — and record what they actually said.</p>
</div>

<div class="note adhd">
  <span class="label">ADHD Observation Tip</span>
  Moving around the room to ask classmates is a good outlet, but it can turn into a social loop. A written target ("two answers recorded before you sit down") gives a clear finish line without relying on verbal reminders.
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Run the Test — Two Tracks Running Concurrently</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Ask and Record</h3>
<p>Students ask at least two people and record their honest answers word-for-word where possible. Anyone who finishes early asks a third person, ideally someone outside their usual friend group.</p>

<div class="choice-moment">
  <span class="cm-label">Choice Moment</span>
  Students choose who to ask — classmates, a teacher, or someone they plan to ask at home tonight. Genuinely open; the best test asks someone who actually has the problem, so a student may reasonably decide to finish the test at home.
</div>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "Would You Pay?"</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">You make bracelets. You ask a friend, "Do you like my bracelet?" They say yes. Does that mean they will buy it?</p>
    <p class="s">[not always / no]</p>
    <p class="t">Right. Saying yes is easy. Paying money is the real test. So ask: "Would you buy this for $5?"</p>
    <p class="a">Practice in pairs: one student holds a simple item, the other role-plays a customer. The seller asks, "Would you buy this for $5?" Then swap. Keep it playful and low-pressure; the goal is practicing the question, not getting a yes.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">One sentence: what is one thing someone told you today that you didn't expect?</p>
  <p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent per student.</p>
</div>

<table class="assess">
  <tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="identity">
  <h3>Asking Takes Courage</h3>
  <p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. Walking up to someone and asking for an honest answer about your own idea isn't easy. You did it anyway. Tomorrow you turn what you learned into one clear statement.</p>
</div>

</div>
