<body>

<header>
  <div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div>
  <h1>My Validated Idea</h1>
  <div class="sub">Week 4 · Day 5 — Portfolio Piece, Preview of Week 5</div>
</header>

<div class="timestrip">
  <div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div>
  <div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Week 4 Recap</div>
  <div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>Write the Three Sentences</div>
  <div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Share Out / SPED Pull-Aside</div>
  <div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Preview Week 5</div>
</div>

<div class="wrap">

<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  Finalizes the Week 4 end-of-week artifact — one validated idea in three sentences: the problem, my idea, and the reason my customers choose me. It goes into the running Week 1–9 workbook section and becomes the foundation for Week 5's Value Proposition work.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Week 4 Recap</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">This week: hypothesis means?</p>
  <p class="cr">"A guess you haven't tested yet."</p>
  <p class="t">MVP?</p>
  <p class="cr">"The smallest version that still tests the idea."</p>
  <p class="t">Pivot?</p>
  <p class="cr">"Changing direction because of what you learned."</p>
  <p class="t">Today, you turn what you learned into three clear sentences.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — The Three Sentences</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — Modeling the Three Sentences</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Three sentences. One: the problem. Two: my idea. Three: the reason my customers choose me. Watch me build mine from my test results.</p>
  <p class="a">Model with your own example: "Elders in this community often can't get to appointments when family isn't available. My idea is a volunteer ride sign-up board run through the senior center. Customers choose it because it's free, local, and run by people they already know."</p>
  <p class="t">Notice the third sentence uses something I actually learned in my test. It isn't a guess.</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">We Do — Strengthening the Third Sentence</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Read this third sentence with me: "Customers choose it because it's good." Strong or weak?</p>
  <p class="s">[weak — not specific]</p>
  <p class="t">Right. Good how? Compared to what? What did people actually tell you in your test?</p>
  <p class="a">Have students offer specific replacements. Point back to what people said on Day 4 — the strongest "reason customers choose me" comes from real answers, not from what the student hoped they would say.</p>
</div>

<div class="frame">
  Sentence 1 — The problem: ______________________________<br>
  Sentence 2 — My idea: ______________________________<br>
  Sentence 3 — Customers choose me because: ______________________________
</div>

<div class="note comprehension">
  <span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>
  Check whether sentence 3 connects to something a real person actually said during the test, or whether the student wrote a hope. If a test result didn't support the idea, that's a valid finding — encourage a pivot statement in sentence 2 rather than ignoring the result.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">You Do — Final Write</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Write your three sentences. If your test told you something surprising, let it change your idea — that's the whole point of testing.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Voluntary Share-Out / Two Tracks</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Optional Share</h3>
<p>Ask for 3–4 volunteers to read their three sentences aloud. Not mandatory.</p>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — Finishing the Week</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">This week you learned to try a little first, ask real questions, and change your plan if you need to. Let's put your idea in words. The problem is ____. My idea is ____. People would pay because ____.</p>
    <p class="a">Help each student say it out loud first, then support them writing or dictating it. Accept simple answers; the goal is the habit of naming problem, idea, and reason.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check + Preview Week 5</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Before you log off — next week we work on the words and plan for getting your idea in front of the right people. Who is it really for, and how will they find out about it? Bring your three sentences Monday.</p>
  <p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent — final Week 4 mark, based on the finished artifact's specificity and use of test results.</p>
</div>

<table class="assess">
  <tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="identity">
  <h3>This Idea Has Been Tested</h3>
  <p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. Most people never test their ideas. You did. What's in your workbook now isn't a guess anymore — it's something you checked with real people. You have everything you need to keep building it.</p>
</div>

</div>
